{"hash":"bbde13c2e5cd1ee9","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":102.882,"said":"Bend down at the water’s edge and look at the stones in the shallows, rounded and smoothed by the current. Along this river there is an old saying: ‘Narmada ke kankar, utne Shankar.’ As many pebbles as the Narmada holds, so many Shankars. Every stone of this river, people believe, is Shiva himself; tradition says the Narmada was granted a boon that every pebble of her bed would be called Shankar. That is why, in the temple lanes and on the ghat steps, you will see stalls heaped with glossy, egg-shaped stones, cream and brown, some marked with paler bands. These are bana lingas, also called Narmadeshwar. A linga shaped by the river rather than by any hand is honoured as swayambhu, self-born, and so a Narmadeshwar linga is commonly installed for worship without the prana-pratishtha rite that a carved image needs. To be truthful, stones shaped wholly by the river are rarer now, and most you will see are cut from Narmada stone and polished by craftsmen. Many come from Bakawan, a village on the river not far from here, where boatmen’s families of the Kewat community began this work about a hundred years ago; today their lingas go to temples across India and abroad. Whether the river or a craftsman’s patient hands gave it shape, the stone is still the Narmada’s. If you take one home, carry it with reverence. On this river, even a pebble is worshipped.\n\nDid you know? Bana lingas are hard stones, about 7 on the Mohs scale, as hard as quartz, which is why the river can polish them for ages without wearing them away.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1367},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1383,"end":1532}],"words":[[0.098,0.337,0,4],[0.447,0.325,5,9],[0.783,0.132,10,12],[0.927,0.096,13,16],[1.036,0.517,17,24],[1.564,0.3,25,29],[1.877,0.228,30,33],[2.117,0.192,34,38],[2.322,0.108,39,41],[2.442,0.096,42,45],[2.55,0.433,46,52],[2.995,0.096,53,55],[3.103,0.096,56,59],[3.211,